  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nUpper low from remnants of TC Isaias centered across western TN with \nthe bulk of the moisture surging northeast. Across the area, \ninverted trough/weak deformation zone has allowed for rather \nlackluster development of showers with a few spots seeing trace \namounts or maybe a few hundredths. While a small window still exists \nover the next 6 to 9 hours for a bit more coverage, doesn't look \noverly promising with pops lowered into chc range. Any precip should \nexit by afternoon, but clouds are likely to linger in eastern areas \nwhich will limit high temperatures in these areas. \n\n925 mb moisture will be slow to depart this evening and into the \novernight hours. If clearing can happen fast enough overnight in \neastern areas, overnight lows should drop below crossover temps with \nat least some patchy fog developing. Although these factors seem \nlogical, lack of rainfall may limit the boundary layer moisture \nsomewhat. Will leave patchy mention for now. \n\nYet another strong surge of WAA commences Monday and Tuesday ahead \nof a wave. Main sfc low tracks north of the area with a cool front \ntrying to move into northern areas, which will bring somewhat of a \ntemp gradient with temps well in the 80s south and 70s north. Given \nthe low track will be north and moisture somewhat limited, any low \nend pops will be confined mainly in lower MI. The frontal boundary \nwill drift south Wed into Thur which may bring slightly better \nchances for showers as waves travel west to east. Models continue to \nvary on whether northern and southern stream troughs will phase with \nGFS/Canadian phasing while EC keep main southern stream wave cutoff \nto the west. This not only brings uncertainty to precip chances, but \nalso the degree of CAA in the wake of the cold front on Friday.",
